---
import SiteNav from "../../components/SiteNav.astro";
import BaseHead from "../../components/BaseHead.astro";
import TableOfContents from "../../components/TableOfContents.astro";
import DocSection from "../../components/docs/DocSection.astro";
import DocAlert from "../../components/docs/DocAlert.astro";
import DocNavigation from "../../components/docs/DocNavigation.astro";
import "../../styles/global.css";
import { wasmSectionPages } from "../../data/wasm-pages";

const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: "overview", text: "Overview" },
  {
    depth: 2,
    slug: "contenteditable-with-wasm",
    text: "contenteditable + WASM hybrid",
  },
  { depth: 2, slug: "source-of-truth", text: "Source of truth" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "event-pipeline", text: "Input events & order" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "reconciliation", text: "DOM ↔ model loop" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "normalization", text: "DOM shape & schema" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "islands", text: "Non-editable islands" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "wasm-calls", text: "When to call WASM" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "canvas-custom", text: "Canvas / custom renderers" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "where-rust-sits", text: "Where Rust usually sits" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "choosing", text: "Choosing & next steps" },
];

const navPages = wasmSectionPages.map((p) => ({
  title: p.titleEn,
  href: `/wasm/${p.slug}`,
  description: p.descEn,
}));
const currentPath = "/wasm/editing-approaches";
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <BaseHead
      title="Editing approaches (Rust + WASM) – contenteditable.lab"
      description="Using contenteditable with a Rust/WASM core: single source of truth, beforeinput and composition, reconciling DOM to model, normalization, and efficient boundary calls."
    />
  </head>
  <body>
    <SiteNav />
    <main class="flex max-w-full m-0 p-0">
      <section
        class="flex-1 flex max-w-[1120px] mx-auto py-7 px-5 pb-10 gap-6 w-full"
      >
        <aside class="hidden lg:block w-[220px] shrink-0">
          <TableOfContents headings={headings} />
        </aside>
        <div class="flex-1 min-w-0">
          <header class="mb-8">
            <nav class="mb-4 text-sm">
              <a href="/" class="text-text-muted no-underline">Home</a>
              <span class="text-text-faint mx-2">/</span>
              <a href="/wasm" class="text-text-muted no-underline">Wasm</a>
              <span class="text-text-faint mx-2">/</span>
              <span class="text-text-primary">Editing approaches</span>
            </nav>
            <h1 class="text-3xl leading-tight m-0 mb-2">Editing approaches</h1>
            <p class="m-0 text-[0.95rem] text-text-secondary">
              How to combine <code>contenteditable</code> with a Rust/WASM document
              core so IME, selection, and your model stay coherent.
            </p>
          </header>

          <article
            class="rounded-xl border border-border-light bg-bg-surface p-6 px-7 text-[0.9rem] leading-[1.7]"
          >
            <DocSection id="overview" title="Overview">
              <p class="m-0 mb-4">
                Most web editors that ship a WASM “brain” still use the browser’s
                editable surface: a <code>contenteditable</code> root (or a thin
                wrapper). Rust handles validation, transforms, collaboration, and
                heavy parsing; the DOM handles caret, IME, and native selection.
              </p>
              <p class="m-0 mb-4">
                This page details <strong>policies and loops</strong> that keep that
                split healthy. For general model/view theory see{" "}
                <a href="/editor/architecture" class="text-accent-primary underline"
                  >Editor → Architecture</a
                >; for IME and clipboard specifics see the linked Wasm guides.
              </p>
            </DocSection>

            <DocSection
              id="contenteditable-with-wasm"
              title="contenteditable + WASM hybrid"
            >
              <p class="m-0 mb-4">
                In the hybrid pattern, <strong>JavaScript</strong> (or TS) owns the
                DOM tree the user types into: event listeners, optional virtual
                layer, and applying patches back from Rust. <strong>WASM</strong>{" "}
                owns the canonical document (or op log) you trust for undo,
                collaboration, and export.
              </p>
              <ul class="m-0 mb-4 pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                  <strong>Pros:</strong> IME, spellcheck integration, accessibility
                  tree, and platform selection behavior come from the browser.
                </li>
                <li>
                  <strong>Cons:</strong> Every browser mutates HTML slightly; you
                  must reconcile continuously. Our{" "}
                  <a href="/scenarios" class="text-accent-primary underline"
                    >Scenarios</a
                  > document real cross-browser differences.
                </li>
              </ul>
              <DocAlert type="info">
                <p class="m-0 text-sm">
                  Rust does not replace the editable surface here—it replaces{" "}
                  <strong>fragile or expensive</strong> document logic you prefer
                  to keep deterministic and testable in one language.
                </p>
              </DocAlert>
            </DocSection>

            <DocSection id="source-of-truth" title="Source of truth">
              <p class="m-0 mb-4">
                Pick <strong>one</strong> authority for committed text at any moment:
              </p>
              <ul class="m-0 mb-4 pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                  <strong>Model-led:</strong> After each stable edit (often after
                  IME <code>compositionend</code>), WASM applies an op; JS updates
                  the DOM to match. Risk: fighting the browser if you patch mid-composition.
                </li>
                <li>
                  <strong>DOM-led during typing:</strong> While the user edits, the
                  DOM is truth; you periodically or on commit parse into the Rust
                  model. Risk: drift if parsing is lossy—tighten schema and tests.
                </li>
              </ul>
              <p class="m-0 mb-4">
                A practical split: treat the DOM as truth <strong>during an IME
                composition session</strong>, then commit a single stable update into
                Rust (see{" "}
                <a href="/wasm/ime-and-composition" class="text-accent-primary underline"
                  >IME & composition</a
                >).
              </p>
              <DocAlert type="warning">
                <p class="m-0 text-sm">
                  Two competing “truths” (full DOM string and full Rust tree updated
                  independently every keystroke) causes races and duplicate
                  characters—design the handoff explicitly.
                </p>
              </DocAlert>
            </DocSection>

            <DocSection id="event-pipeline" title="Input events & order">
              <p class="m-0 mb-4">
                Build a clear pipeline in your controller code (not implicitly
                across scattered handlers):
              </p>
              <ol class="m-0 mb-4 pl-6 space-y-2 list-decimal">
                <li>
                  <code>beforeinput</code> — intercept or cancel certain edits when
                  the platform allows; read <code>getTargetRanges()</code> when
                  available.
                </li>
                <li>
                  <code>input</code> — DOM has changed; good place for debounced
                  sync <strong>outside</strong> active composition if you use
                  DOM-led snapshots.
                </li>
                <li>
                  <code>composition*</code> — gate when Rust sees committed text vs
                  preview.
                </li>
                <li>
                  <code>selectionchange</code> — map selection to model coordinates;
                  watch for async WASM updates that might invalidate ranges (
                  <a href="/wasm/selection-range-offsets" class="text-accent-primary underline"
                    >Selection & offsets</a
                  >).
                </li>
              </ol>
              <p class="m-0">
                Deeper event semantics:{" "}
                <a href="/editor/input-handling" class="text-accent-primary underline"
                  >Editor → Input handling</a
                >.
              </p>
            </DocSection>

            <DocSection id="reconciliation" title="DOM ↔ model loop">
              <p class="m-0 mb-4">
                Typical loops look like one of:
              </p>
              <ul class="m-0 mb-4 pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                  <strong>Parse on commit:</strong> Read serialized subtree or diff
                  the DOM → send structured ops to WASM → optional WASM response
                  patches DOM if model rejects or normalizes.
                </li>
                <li>
                  <strong>Op stream from JS:</strong> Translate <code>beforeinput</code>{" "}
                  input types into small ops in JS; WASM applies and returns “render
                  instructions” for minimal DOM updates.
                </li>
                <li>
                  <strong>Projection:</strong> WASM is authoritative; after each
                  transaction, JS replaces or patches the editable subtree from a
                  serialized view (more control, more diff cost).
                </li>
              </ul>
              <p class="m-0 mb-4">
                Avoid shipping the <strong>entire HTML string</strong> across the
                boundary on every event unless profiling proves it is cheap enough (
                <a href="/wasm/js-wasm-boundary" class="text-accent-primary underline"
                  >JS ↔ WASM boundary</a
                >).
              </p>
              <p class="m-0">
                Model/DOM theory:{" "}
                <a
                  href="/editor/model-dom-synchronization"
                  class="text-accent-primary underline"
                  >Editor → Model–DOM synchronization</a
                >.
              </p>
            </DocSection>

            <DocSection id="normalization" title="DOM shape & schema">
              <p class="m-0 mb-4">
                Browsers insert <code>&lt;br&gt;</code>, empty blocks, and nested
                spans differently. Your Rust schema should define what is{" "}
                <strong>valid</strong>; after paste or Enter, normalize either in JS
                (fast DOM surgery) or WASM (shared rules with native clients).
              </p>
              <ul class="m-0 mb-4 pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                  Run normalization <strong>after</strong> clipboard insert and{" "}
                  <strong>before</strong> persisting a CRDT update if paste can
                  introduce forbidden tags (
                  <a href="/wasm/clipboard-input-routing" class="text-accent-primary underline"
                    >Clipboard & input routing</a
                  >).
                </li>
                <li>
                  Keep one policy with your sanitizer to avoid XSS divergence (
                  <a href="/wasm/security-deployment-wasm" class="text-accent-primary underline"
                    >Security & deployment</a
                  >).
                </li>
              </ul>
              <p class="m-0">
                Schema concepts:{" "}
                <a href="/editor/model-schema" class="text-accent-primary underline"
                  >Editor → Model & schema</a
                >,{" "}
                <a href="/editor/html-mapping" class="text-accent-primary underline"
                  >HTML mapping</a
                >.
              </p>
            </DocSection>

            <DocSection id="islands" title="Non-editable islands">
              <p class="m-0 mb-4">
                Mixing <code>contenteditable=false</code> nodes, mentions, math
                placeholders, or custom widgets inside the editor surface is common.
                Selection can collapse or jump when crossing boundaries—behavior
                differs on Android vs desktop.
              </p>
              <p class="m-0 mb-4">
                WASM doesn’t fix this: your <strong>view layer</strong> must define
                click targets, keyboard traps, and how selection maps into the Rust
                model (skip widgets vs editable text).
              </p>
              <p class="m-0">
                Related:{" "}
                <a href="/wasm/accessibility-wasm" class="text-accent-primary underline"
                  >Accessibility</a
                >, site cases on non-editable regions.
              </p>
            </DocSection>

            <DocSection id="wasm-calls" title="When to call WASM">
              <p class="m-0 mb-4">
                Practical rules of thumb:
              </p>
              <ul class="m-0 mb-4 pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                  <strong>Do call</strong> for schema validation, transform apply,
                  paste HTML parse, CRDT merge, export/import, expensive diff.
                </li>
                <li>
                  <strong>Avoid calling</strong> on every <code>compositionupdate</code>{" "}
                  unless you measured the need for live preview in the model.
                </li>
                <li>
                  <strong>Batch</strong> rapid typing into frame-aligned or
                  micro-task batches if you use DOM snapshots—never reorder relative
                  to IME commit order.
                </li>
              </ul>
            </DocSection>

            <DocSection id="canvas-custom" title="Canvas / custom renderers">
              <p class="m-0 mb-4">
                Some products render body text on Canvas/WebGL but still keep a
                hidden <code>contenteditable</code> for IME. Others go fully custom;
                that shifts IME and accessibility work onto you.
              </p>
              <p class="m-0 mb-4">
                Prefer staying DOM-hosted until you have a concrete requirement
                (print-accurate pagination, game UI) that outweighs the cost.
              </p>
            </DocSection>

            <DocSection id="where-rust-sits" title="Where Rust usually sits">
              <ul class="m-0 pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                  Document model + operations (apply transforms, enforce schema).
                </li>
                <li>
                  Parsing / normalization of pasted or imported HTML; markdown
                  round-trips.
                </li>
                <li>
                  CRDT / sync (e.g. Yrs) in WASM next to a JS editor host (
                  <a href="/wasm/collaboration-crdt-wasm" class="text-accent-primary underline"
                    >Collaboration & CRDT</a
                  >).
                </li>
                <li>
                  Optional layout when you don’t rely on the browser for line breaks
                  in the body (advanced).
                </li>
              </ul>
            </DocSection>

            <DocSection id="choosing" title="Choosing & next steps">
              <p class="m-0 mb-4">
                Default path: <strong>contenteditable surface + WASM core</strong>,
                explicit source-of-truth rules, and a reconciliation loop you can
                trace in the debugger. Measure boundary traffic before optimizing
                rendering.
              </p>
              <p class="m-0 mb-4">
                Read next:{" "}
                <a href="/wasm/ime-and-composition" class="text-accent-primary font-medium underline"
                  >IME & composition</a
                >
                ·
                <a href="/wasm/js-wasm-boundary" class="text-accent-primary font-medium underline"
                  >JS ↔ WASM boundary</a
                >
                ·
                <a href="/wasm/undo-model" class="text-accent-primary font-medium underline"
                  >Undo & redo model</a
                >
              </p>
            </DocSection>

            <DocNavigation
              current={currentPath}
              pages={navPages}
              title="Wasm guides"
            />
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
